{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a56a7c280325f2ff","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Epic Creator and Decomposer: Human Review Guide\n2\t\n3\t*For engineers, SMEs, and architects looking at the epics this pipeline creates.*\n4\t\n5\t**In short:** twice a day, this pipeline reads the RHAISTRAT strategies that have\n6\tpassed the strategy rubric, been signed off by a human, and are targeted at 3.6,\n7\tand turns each one into\n8\ta reviewed **proposal** — a dependency graph (DAG) of implementation epics created\n9\tin the **RHAI** Jira project. Every breakdown clears an adversarial quality review\n10\t*before* a single epic is created, so what lands in Jira is something we stand\n11\tbehind. Every epic it creates carries the label `epic-creator-auto-created`.\n12\t\n13\tStart with the epic DAG for your strategy. If something doesn't fit your plan,\n14\tadjust it — and please tell us in **#wg-rhai-epic-and-code-gen** so we can keep\n15\tmaking it better.\n16\t\n17\t| Repo | What it is |\n18\t|---|---|\n19\t| [`epic-creator`](https://github.com/opendatahub-io/epic-creator) | The brain — the skill and prompts that do the decomposition. |\n20\t| [`epic-decomposer`](https://gitlab.com/redhat/rhel-ai/agentic-ci/epic-decomposer) | The runner — the CI job that runs it against Jira twice a day. |\n21\t| [`epic-decomposer-results`](https://gitlab.com/redhat/rhel-ai/agentic-ci/epic-decomposer-results) | The results store — every run's epics, reviews, and a dashboard, versioned by timestamp. |\n22\t\n23\t## How it works\n24\t\n25\tTwice a day the pipeline:\n26\t\n27\t1. **Reads** the signed-off strategies that qualify (see below).\n28\t2. **Decomposes** each into a DAG of epics — Implementation and Investigation\n29\t   epics, with dependency links between them.\n30\t3. **Reviews** the breakdown adversarially and scores it out of 14. If it doesn't\n31\t   pass, it's revised and re-reviewed. **Nothing is created in Jira until the\n32\t   review passes.**\n33\t4. **Creates** the epics in **RHAI**, wired together with \"Blocks\" dependency links.\n34\t5. **Publishes** a visual DAG and an HTML dashboard to `epic-decomposer-results`.\n35\t\n36\tAs a reader you mostly care about three things: the **epic list** for your\n37\tstrategy, each epic's **acceptance criteria**, and the **rendered DAG** that shows\n38\thow the epics depend on one another. The easiest place to see all three is the\n39\t**[dashboard](https://epic-decomposer-dashboard-4e1d18.gitlab.io/)** — open the\n40\t**Strategy Tracker** tab and find your RHAISTRAT. The same content also lives in\n41\t`epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-<id>-decomposition.md` in the results repo.\n42\t\n43\t> **Coming soon:** we'll be integrating this with **Org Pulse**, so you'll be able\n44\t> to see the decomposed epics alongside the rest of your team's work there too.\n45\t\n46\t> **Note:** this only generates *epics* right now — we are **not** generating code\n47\t> automatically yet. This is still a work in progress and we're experimenting with\n48\t> a small number of epics. If you have an epic that's boring or small and you'd\n49\t> like us to give it a try, let us know in **#wg-rhai-epic-and-code-gen**.\n50\t\n51\t## Which strategies we generate epics for\n52\t\n53\tA strategy enters the pipeline when it is a **RHAISTRAT Feature** that is:\n54\t\n55\t> labeled **`strat-creator-rubric-pass`** **and** **`strat-creator-human-sign-off`**,\n56\t> with a **Target Version of 3.6** (any of the 3.6 EA/GA values).\n57\t\n58\tIn plain terms: it passed the strategy rubric, a human signed off on it, and it's\n59\ttargeted at 3.6. That target-version filter is the one thing we bump each release —\n60\tright now it's 3.6 only.\n61\t\n62\t## When we don't create epics\n63\t\n64\tWe deliberately stay out of the way in these cases:\n65\t\n66\t- **It already has epics.** If the strategy already has child Epics (or a legacy\n67\t  \"Incorporates\" link), we skip it and never re-decompose — your existing\n68\t  breakdown wins.\n69\t- **The quality review didn't pass.** A breakdown has to score **≥ 10 out of 14\n70\t  with no criterion scoring zero**. If it doesn't clear that bar, nothing is\n71\t  submitted to Jira. \n72\t- **It's below the threshold.** Small strategies (S-sized, one component, one\n73\t  team, mostly high-confidence work) collapse to a single epic instead of a DAG.\n74\t- **It's documentation only.** If every affected component is \"no code changes\" or\n75\t  \"reference only,\" you get one docs-authoring epic.\n76\t- **A component is passive.** A component that only needs to keep working (no code\n77\t  changes) becomes an acceptance criterion on the nearest active epic rather than\n78\t  its own epic — unless a *different* team has to validate it, in which case it\n79\t  gets its own validation epic.\n80\t\n81\t## Investigation epics\n82\t\n83\tEpics come in two types:\n84\t\n85\t- **Implementation** — produces an artifact (code, config, docs, manifests, an\n86\t  upstream PR, and so on).\n87\t- **Investigation** — resolves an uncertainty that a downstream decision depends\n88\t  on.\n89\t\n90\tThe line between \"this deserves its own Investigation epic\" and \"this is just an\n91\tacceptance criterion\" comes down to one question:\n92\t\n93\t> **Does the answer change which downstream epics exist or what they do?**\n94\t> **Yes** → it's an Investigation epic, and the epics that depend on its outcome\n95\t> are gated on it. **No** → it's an acceptance criterion on an Implementation epic.\n96\t\n97\tBecause an investigation is inherently more open-ended than implementation work,\n98\tit's routed by its own scoring: **High** → handled by the AI investigation skill,\n99\t**Medium** → a hybrid where the skill does the desk/local work and hands a spec to\n100\ta human, **Low** → assigned to a person.\n101\t\n102\t> **Heads up:** investigation epics aren't 100% automated yet. If you land on one\n103\t> and aren't sure how to take it forward, **ping us in #wg-rhai-epic-and-code-gen**\n104\t> and we'll help.\n105\t\n106\t## Labels\n107\t\n108\tEvery epic and strategy the pipeline touches is labeled so you can find and filter\n109\tthem.\n110\t\n111\t**On each auto-created epic (in RHAI):**\n112\t\n113\t| Label | Meaning |\n114\t|---|---|\n115\t| `epic-creator-auto-created` | Always applied — marks every epic this pipeline created. |\n116\t| `epic-creator-investigation` | The epic is an Investigation (vs. Implementation). |\n117\t| `epic-creator-impl-<type>` | Implementation subtype, e.g. `epic-creator-impl-docs-authoring`. |\n118\t| `epic-creator-ai-impl-*` | The epic's AI-implementability class — `high`, `medium`, or `low`. |\n119\t| `epic-creator-needs-component` | The component isn't in the canonical RHAI component list yet. |\n120\t\n121\t**On the source strategy (in RHAISTRAT):**\n122\t\n123\t| Label | Meaning |\n124\t|---|---|\n125\t| `epic-creator-auto-decomposed` | Applied only when **all** of the strategy's epics were successfully created. |\n126\t\n127\tThe two labels that decide whether a strategy *enters* the pipeline —\n128\t`strat-creator-rubric-pass` and `strat-creator-human-sign-off` — come from the\n129\tupstream strat-creator pipeline, not this one. And `epic-creator-ai-impl-*` simply\n130\treflects the AI-implementability class described in the Advanced section below.\n131\t\n132\t## If an epic isn't right — what to do\n133\t\n134\tThe breakdown is a reviewed proposal, but you own your delivery. Here's the flow:\n135\t\n136\t1. **Start from the visual DAG** for your strategy — open the\n137\t   **[dashboard](https://epic-decomposer-dashboard-4e1d18.gitlab.io/)** and its\n138\t   **Strategy Tracker** tab. It shows every epic, its type, and how they depend on\n139\t   each other at a glance.\n140\t   That's the fastest way to sanity-check the whole shape before looking at any\n141\t   single epic.\n142\t2. **If an epic is wrong or unnecessary, just close it.** No ceremony required.\n143\t3. **Create your own epics in RHOAIENG** to complement (or replace) these if you\n144\t   need to. **RHAI is the AI-generated project**; **RHOAIENG** is the normal\n145\t   engineering backlog where humans track real delivery work.\n146\t4. **Tell us in #wg-rhai-epic-and-code-gen.** Two things help us the most:\n147\t   - whether the epic breakdown and its DAG were correct, and\n148\t   - if you created your own epics, *what* you created and *why* the generated\n149\t     ones didn't fit.\n150\t\n151\t   That feedback is exactly how the pipeline keeps getting better.\n152\t\n153\t> **Please keep in mind:** a slight rename of an epic title is totally fine. But if you\n154\t> want to change an epic's content or its acceptance criteria, please talk to us in\n155\t> **#wg-rhai-epic-and-code-gen** first — those are what we learn from, and quietly\n156\t> editing them makes it hard for us to tell whether the breakdown was right.\n157\t\n158\t## Resources\n159\t\n160\t- **Dashboard** — [epic-decomposer-dashboard](https://epic-decomposer-dashboard-4e1d18.gitlab.io/)\n161\t  (see the **Strategy Tracker** tab). Raw per-run artifacts live in\n162\t  `epic-decomposer-results`.\n163\t- **Per-strategy DAG + traceability** — `epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-<id>-decomposition.md`.\n164\t- **How a run was scored** — `epic-reviews/RHAISTRAT-<id>-decomp-review.md`.\n165\t- **Feedback & questions** — **#wg-rhai-epic-and-code-gen**.\n166\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-985c5dfd08bf1e09","agent_id":"a78871ae114a39812","session_id":"44bb4be5-e725-48c0-870c-d3c41c68193e","uuid":"6b6daf39-1a3f-41d5-b3cc-b11c052ba7e1","timestamp":"2026-10-26T22:08:16.020Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a56a7c280325f2ff","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9013"}
