{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a3a8d1df8bef8821","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9013: FIPS 140-3 compliance for the model serving stack\n11\t\n12\tStrategy status: Refinement Done. Strategy priority: Critical.\n13\t\n14\tTriage: full decomposition. The strategy is not below-threshold — it affects two\n15\tcomponents (KServe, Platform Operator), spans architecturally distinct work\n16\tstreams (image code conversion, a compliance evidence process with external\n17\tsubmission, and documentation authoring), and is Critical priority. It is not\n18\tdocs-only — HLR-1 requires code changes to serving-stack images.\n19\t\n20\t## Epic List\n21\t\n22\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n23\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 | FIPS 140-3 conversion of KServe serving-stack images | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 | FIPS 140-3 conversion of Platform Operator images | Implementation | RHOAI Platform | P0 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 | FIPS 140-3 compliance evidence package and certification submission | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 | Documentation of the FIPS support posture and cluster prerequisites | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n28\t\n29\t## Dependency DAG\n30\t\n31\t```mermaid\n32\tgraph TD\n33\t    E001[\"E001 KServe image FIPS conversion\"] --> E004[\"E004 FIPS posture documentation\"]\n34\t    E002[\"E002 Platform Operator image FIPS conversion\"] --> E004\n35\t    E003[\"E003 Compliance evidence package and submission\"] --> E004\n36\t```\n37\t\n38\tE001, E002, and E003 are parallel roots; E004 is blocked by all three.\n39\t\n40\t## DAG Justification\n41\t\n42\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n43\t|------|------|-----------|\n44\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring epic is blocked by every Implementation epic — docs describe what was built. |\n45\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 | Same — docs-last rule. |\n46\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 | Same — docs-last rule. |\n47\t| no edge E001 ↔ E002 | Rules 1, 14 | Different components and repos, no shared build artifacts → parallel. |\n48\t| no edge E001/E002 ↔ E003 | Rule 13 (content-consumption clarification) | The evidence package consumes build attestations and test results as **content**, not as artifacts its code builds against. Rule 13 is explicit that build outputs consumed as content (attestations, scan results, test evidence) are AC gates on the consuming epic, never DAG edges. E003 therefore runs in parallel with the conversion work and is not complete until its inclusion ACs hold. |\n49\t| no edge for the external lab | Rule 21 / Step 5.5 | The accredited laboratory and security review board are third-party **process** gates, not engineering artifacts — no upstream PR/RFC exists to model as an epic. Modeled as tiered delivery (below) plus risk notes on E003. |\n50\t| no Investigation epics | Step 3 | The strategy states \"None on scope\" for open questions; no risks, assumptions, pending reviews, or conditional ADRs are recorded. Which specific images link non-validated crypto is unresolved, but the answer does not change which epics exist or what they do — it is the audit work inside each conversion epic (an acceptance criterion, per the Step 3 decision rule). |\n51\t\n52\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n53\t\n54\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n55\t|-----|----------|---------|\n56\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 (KServe images), E002 (Platform Operator images) — split by component per Epic Boundary Rule 1. |\n57\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E003 (full). |\n58\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E001 (bundled), E002 (bundled) — the CI FIPS regression check is a shared-surface enforcement deliverable: it enforces over exactly the images each conversion epic converts, so it is divided per surface (each conversion epic authors and verifies the check covering its own image builds) rather than carved into a standalone work-type epic. A standalone check epic would build the same enforcement surface twice and add an artificial serialization edge. |\n59\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E004 (docs-authoring). |\n60\t\n61\tFull coverage: HLR-1 through HLR-4 are all mapped; no orphaned requirements.\n62\t\n63\tNote on E004 priority: HLR-4 is P2, but docs-authoring epics take their priority\n64\tfrom the strategy frontmatter (`priority: Critical` → P0) per the docs-authoring\n65\tpriority exception, and do not push priority upstream.\n66\t\n67\t## Component and Team Mapping\n68\t\n69\t- `KServe` and `Platform Operator` are both verbatim canonical component names from `.context/rhai-components.txt`.\n70\t- E003 (evidence package) spans both strategy components; assigned `KServe` as the single most-relevant canonical name because the compliance scope is the model serving stack, with the span noted here.\n71\t- E004 is assigned the canonical `Documentation` component.\n72\t- Teams: KServe ownership (RHOAI Serving) comes from `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`. The strategy has no Impacted Teams table; the Platform Operator team (RHOAI Platform) and the docs epic team are inferred — flagged in Health Warnings.\n73\t\n74\t## Health Warnings\n75\t\n76\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, no NFRs, and no Staff Engineer Input. Decomposition proceeded from the HLR table, Affected Components list, and problem statement. Team assignments for E002 and E004 are inferred and should be verified by a human.\n77\t2. **No architecture-context file for Platform Operator**: the provisioned architecture-context snapshot contains only `rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`. Platform Operator is treated as an **existing** component — it is in the canonical RHAI component list and the strategy's premise is that its images already exist and link non-validated crypto (you cannot FIPS-convert images of a component that does not exist) — so no new-component onboarding chain (repo/license/konflux) was generated. The `architecture_claims` signal for E002 is scored 0 (no context file covers it). A human should confirm the operator's repo and image layout.\n78\t3. **Externally controlled schedule gates**: laboratory turnaround (historically six to nine months) and security review board cadence are outside engineering control. Per the AC rules these are recorded as Risks/Notes on E003, not as acceptance criteria; E003's ACs sit on the engineering completion point (package assembled, submitted, triaged).\n79\t4. **Scope ambiguity — \"serving data plane\"**: the problem statement says \"KServe components and the serving data plane\" link non-validated crypto. Per the architecture context, model execution in the serving stack involves Serving Runtimes (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit). The Affected Components list names only KServe and Platform Operator, so the decomposition covers those two; if Serving Runtimes images are intended in scope, that is a strategy amendment requiring additional epics. Flagged for human confirmation.\n80\t5. **Priority note**: HLR-4 is P2 while its epic E004 carries P0 — this is the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter Critical → P0), not an inversion. HLR-3 (P1) is bundled into the P0 conversion epics because its enforcement surface is identical to their conversion surface (recorded in the traceability matrix row above).\n81\t\n82\t## Tiered Delivery\n83\t\n84\tThe external dependency with uncertain timing is the certification laboratory\n85\t(and the review board cadence). Partial value is deliverable without it, so the\n86\tstrategy splits naturally:\n87\t\n88\t- **Tier 1 (independent value)**: E001 and E002 — the serving stack runs\n89\t  correctly on FIPS-enabled OpenShift clusters using validated crypto modules,\n90\t  with CI regression enforcement (HLR-1, HLR-3). This is complete regardless of\n91\t  certification timing.\n92\t- **Tier 2 (externally gated)**: E003 — the evidence package is assembled and\n93\t  submitted (HLR-2); the certification *outcome* arrives on the lab's schedule\n94\t  after submission. E004 (HLR-4) follows all implementation work per the\n95\t  docs-last rule and documents the Tier 1 posture.\n96\t\n97\tNo DAG edge models the lab: it is a process gate on the certification outcome,\n98\tnot a prerequisite for any engineering work (Rule 21 — third party resolves →\n99\tprecondition with tiered delivery, no separate epic).\n100\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a9638a8823e80ba9","agent_id":"a044fc504d2e0fe1d","session_id":"44bb4be5-e725-48c0-870c-d3c41c68193e","uuid":"44291d47-9072-4071-b8bf-ee10c093f5a3","timestamp":"2026-10-26T22:11:58.720Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a3a8d1df8bef8821","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9013 decomposition"}
